  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "K.Abdulla vs The Food Inspector on 1 February, 2017 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Wednesday, The 1St Day Of February 2017/12Th Magha, 1938 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 1244 of 2012 () -------------------------------- Judgment In Cra 760/2008 of Addl.Sessions Court (Adhoc)-Ii, Kozhikode And In In Cc 346/2006 of Jmfc, Payyoli Revision Petitioner(S): ---------------------- K.Abdulla, S/O Ammed, Kunnath House, Payyoli Bazar, Payyoli, Koyilandy Taluk, Kozhikode District By Adv. Sri.Sunny Mathew Respondent(S): -------------- 1. The Food Inspector, Permabra Circle, Perambra, Kozhikode District 2. State Of Kerala, Represented By The Ppublic Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam By Public Prosecutor Sri. C.K. Prasad This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 01-02-2017, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: B.Sudheendra Kumar, J. - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - -- - - - - - - - - - K.Abdulla vs The Food Inspector on 1 February, 2017",
    "facts": "He was convicted and sentenced by the courts below concurrently for the offence under Section 16(1)(a)(i) read with Section 2(ia)(m) of Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 (for short 'the P.F.A. Act') and Rule 5, Appendix B.A. 20.01 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules (for short 'the P.F.A. Rules') . 2. The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 13-7-2006, PW1 the Food Inspector purchased three bottles of Synthetic Vinegar from the first accused. Crl .R.P.1244 of 2012 The 2nd accused was the licensee of the shop and the 3rd accused was the manufacturer of the food item. After complying with the legal formalities, one sample was sent for analysis to the Public Analyst. After analysis, the Public Analyst issued Ext. P15 report stating that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for synthetic vinegar as per PFA Rules, and was therefore, adulterated. 3. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW5 were examined and Exts.P1 to P30 were marked for the prosecution. DW1 to DW3 were examined and Exts. D1 to D5 series were marked for the defence.",
    "arguments": "The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has argued that since no laboratory for the analysis of the Crl.R.P.1244 of 2012 food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P15 report of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6 It was held by the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report. The Division Bench of this Court in Rasheed v. Food Inspector [2016 (2) KLT 390] considered the above question on a reference by a learned Single Judge. The Division Bench in Rasheed (supra) held that if an analysis is required to initiate prosecution, there should Crl.R.P.1244 of 2012 be notified laboratory and validated method of analysis as mandated under Section 23(1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act for a successful prosecution as held by the Apex Court in Pepsico Holdings (supra).",
    "petitioner_arguments": "The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has argued that since no laboratory for the analysis of the Crl.R.P.1244 of 2012 food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P15 report of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "6 It was held by the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report. The Division Bench of this Court in Rasheed v. Food Inspector [2016 (2) KLT 390] considered the above question on a reference by a learned Single Judge. The Division Bench in Rasheed (supra) held that if an analysis is required to initiate prosecution, there should Crl.R.P.1244 of 2012 be notified laboratory and validated method of analysis as mandated under Section 23(1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act for a successful prosecution as held by the Apex Court in Pepsico Holdings (supra)."
  },
